<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory XIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1502">1502</a>–<a href="/years/1585">1585</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ugo Boncompagni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory XIII was pope from 1572 to 1585. In 1582 he introduced the reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorian-calendar">Gregorian calendar</a>, now used throughout the world, and he founded or endowed many colleges for the training of priests, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>' Roman College, later named the Pontifical Gregorian University in his honour. Banditry and financial strain troubled the Papal States in his last years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory XIII" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Bartolomeo Passarotti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Gregorio_XIII_-_Passarotti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ugo Boncompagni was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he studied law and graduated in 1530. He taught jurisprudence for some years, and his students included the future cardinals Alexander Farnese, Reginald Pole and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Charles Borromeo</a>. At 36 he was summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>, under whom he held several appointments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> made him Cardinal-Priest of San Sisto Vecchio and sent him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>.</p><p>He also served as a legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, forming a close relationship with the king. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a>, the conclave of less than 24 hours chose him on 13 May 1572. He took the name Gregory XIII in homage to Gregory the Great, and many historians attribute the speed of the election to Spanish backing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As pope he set out to reform the Church by carrying out the recommendations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>. He required cardinals to reside in their sees, appointed a committee to update the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index of Forbidden Books</a> and sponsored a new edition of the Corpus juris canonici. The power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> grew under him while that of the cardinals declined. He recognised the Discalced Carmelites as a distinct province in 1580 and gave official status to the Congregation of the Oratory in 1575. He was a generous patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> colleges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and founded seminaries, beginning with the German College.</p><p>In foreign policy he was more concerned with the Protestants than with the Turks. He backed Philip II's plans against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a>, and in 1579 a papal expedition of about 50 soldiers landed in Ireland and took part in the Second Desmond Rebellion, ending in the Smerwick Massacre. After the St Bartholomew's Day Massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in 1572 he held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a>. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tensho-embassy">Tenshō embassy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the first pope to do so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Gregory is best known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorian-calendar">Gregorian calendar</a>, devised by the physician and astronomer Aloysius Lilius and finally modified by the Jesuit Christopher Clavius. The Julian year of 365 days and 6 hours was slightly too long, and over 13 centuries the vernal equinox had slipped to 10 March. By the bull Inter gravissimas of 24 February 1582, the day after Thursday 4 October 1582 became 15 October. Catholic states followed at once or within a year or two, but Protestant Europe took more than a century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and its colonies adopted it in 1752.</p><p>He died on 10 April 1585. To raise funds he had confiscated properties across the Papal States, which enriched his treasury for a time but alienated much of the nobility and caused economic and social disorder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Gregory XIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom saw itself as a bastion of Christendom in the East and was the Crusaders' closest ally: <b>Pope Gregory XIII</b> later wrote that no people came to the Crusaders' aid “with the same enthusiasm, joy and faith as the Armenians did”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gregory XIII</b> introduced the Gregorian calendar in 1582, now used worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory XIII</b>'s reform dropped ten days to realign the calendar with the solar year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1555" aria-label="Bologna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope Gregory XIII"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 3739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 3739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1555">What was here in 1555? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
